A||||14||Fformula88||Rear Brakes on 88 Formula||||1||
Z||000000||Fformula88||03-17-2000||06:46 PM||||I am trying to replace the rear brakes on my 88 Formula, but cannot seem to figure out the caliper on it.  I just cannot seem to get the piston back into the caliper.  I know you got to turn it, but when I do, it just turns.  It won't go back in.  Any help is greatly appreciated.  Thanx!||||reg||1
Z||000001||lawrence||03-17-2000||08:12 PM||||You need to turn and push.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A>  - WebElectric Magazine||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fformula88||03-17-2000||08:31 PM||||Ok... tried that.  It still doesn't seem to go in any.  Will it go in quickly, or does it take a lot of turns to get in?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000003||Fformula88||03-23-2000||05:51 PM||||Anybody have anymore advice?  The pistons still won't go back in ANY!  I can get them to turn, and put a lot of force on them while turning them, but they just won't bottom out.  There is no way I can possibly get new pads into it right now.  Any help is appreciated.  Thanx.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||03-23-2000||06:27 PM||||I think you can remove the e-brake lever and rotate the shaft while pushing in on the piston? Seems like I did something like this on an 84. Different brakes but still the same basic design for e-brake???||||reg||3
Z||000005||SCCA FIERO||03-23-2000||07:10 PM||||I just did the brakes on my 88. All I had to do was turn it about 1/2 a turn and it was bottomed. Didn't need to push while turning or nothing like that. I had to use a screwdriver and a hammer to turn the piston.||||reg||1
Z||000006||TOM||03-23-2000||08:54 PM||||were the brakes worn enough that the cam could have come unscrewed of the piston. Then it probably wont catch to go back it because the shaft is not being held straight. You will probably have to pull the pistons out to see what is going on inside.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fformula88||03-23-2000||10:54 PM||||I can turn them.  And if I turn them the opposite way, they come back out, so they are still connected.  They will then turn back into the caliper until they reach their original position from when I started.  Then they still turn, but won't go any farther.  <BR>||||reg||8
Z||000008||SCCA FIERO||03-23-2000||11:41 PM||||You must have some problem then. Mine turned a 1/4 turn and then they wouldn't turn anymore. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||ChadMan||03-24-2000||08:36 AM||||You guys are scaring the hell out of me.  I have to do a brake job on my 88 (including rears) next month... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">||||reg||9
Z||000010||Fformula88||03-24-2000||04:32 PM||||Is it possible the parking brake mechanism is sticking?  I can move the lever fairly easily, and don't think it is, but I cannot figure out why neither piston will bottom out.  If so, is there any way to free up the mechanism for the parking brake without taking the whole caliper apart?  Any other suggestions?  Thanks.<P>Chadman... Don't be scared... be VERY SCARED...hahaha.  J/K.  I know its my car because I have had problems with anything I have ever done to this car....lol<P>||||reg||8
Z||000011||Fformula88||03-25-2000||03:44 PM||||Just wanted to update everyone, in case someone else runs up against the same problem.  I got the pistons back in.  I had to take the bridge off the caliper and really put preasure on the piston while turning it... but they bottomed out.  Unfortunately, one of the calipers died, and is now leaking fluid all over.  So its rebuild time for that one.  The other appears to be working great!  So one out of two isn't too bad I guess.  Thanks for everyone's advice, and wish me luck on the rebuild!||||reg||1
Z||000012||Boxdin||03-26-2000||10:59 PM||||Make sure that the ratchet, or clutch, or clicker mechanisn, what ever you want to call it, functions properly in the base of the piston. If the shaft will not turn inside the pistom, do not use that part. ||||reg||6
Z||000013||Raydar||03-27-2000||01:56 PM||||FFormula... I would go ahead and rebuild both of them, or buy rebuilts. If you had to force them that much, something may be wrong. The other one may start leaking with just a little encouragement. In any event, whatever you do, you should do to both, so that the braking force is the same on both sides. Just my $.02.  ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Fformula88||03-27-2000||08:16 PM||||Your right Raydar... I decided to closely inspect the non-leaking caliper, and in fact it had leaked a little bit.  So I will be rebuilding both if the pistons are still in good shape.  I took the first apart, and the rubber seal inside had broken in about a one inch piece.||||reg||1
